WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.910
Punjab, around 1500.

2
00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:06.250
Women are often seen as ritually impure.

3
00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:09.680
A widow may face burning
on her husband's pyre.

4
00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:13.440
Guru Nanak asks a question:
why call her inferior,

5
00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:16.170
when from her even kings are born?

6
00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:21.030
Later, women as well as men take
initiation and join the Khalsa.

7
00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:25.780
So if the Gurus taught this, why are
Sikhs still arguing about it today?

8
00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:29.510
In this mission, you'll trace
the Gurus' reforms on a timeline,

9
00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:31.750
from Guru Nanak to the Khalsa.

10
00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:37.360
Then you'll weigh up a bold claim: has
feminism always been part of Sikhism?

11
00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:42.680
Your mission: find out why Sikhs still
debate what equality for women means.
